<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Empires of the Sea</h1><p class="path-tagline">States whose power rested on fleets, ports and sea lanes, from Carthage to the British Empire.</p><p class="path-intro">A thalassocracy is a state whose power rests on control of the sea, its trade routes and its ports rather than on large land territories. This path follows such powers from the ancient Mediterranean to the oceans of the modern world: Carthage and Athens, Srivijaya on the Strait of Malacca and the Chola navy that raided it, Venice on its lagoon, and the Portuguese, Dutch and British, who carried that kind of power across the oceans. Most were in the end worn down by rivals at sea or by powers on land.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/thalassocracy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thalassocracy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Thalassocracy</span><span class="path-step-why">The term for a state built on the sea rather than on land, which needed less bureaucracy than a territorial empire; Srivijaya is the classic example.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A thalassocracy is a state whose power rests primarily on control of the sea, maritime trade routes and ports rather than on large land territories.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/punic-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 814 BCE – 146 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Punic Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A Phoenician colony of Tyre in Tunisia that built a trading empire across the western Mediterranean, until three Punic Wars stripped it of Sicily, Spain and its navy.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Punic Empire, or Carthaginian Empire, was a maritime state centred on Carthage in present-day Tunisia, founded as a colony of Tyre and traditionally dated to 814 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 480 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle of Salamis</span><span class="path-step-why">In 480 BCE the allied Greek fleet, led by Athens under Themistocles, defeated the larger fleet of Xerxes I in the straits off Attica, and Xerxes withdrew to Asia.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Battle of Salamis was a naval battle fought in 480 BCE in the straits between the island of Salamis and the coast of Attica, during the second Persian invasion of Greece.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 900 BCE – 86 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Athens</span><span class="path-step-why">After Salamis the city led the Delian League, which it turned into a tribute-paying empire in the Aegean, until its defeat by Sparta in 404 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/srivijaya-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 650 CE – 1377 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Srivijaya Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From Palembang in Sumatra it ruled the Strait of Malacca from the 7th to the 13th century, growing rich by taxing the shipping between China and India.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Srivijaya was a maritime empire based at Palembang in Sumatra from the seventh century to around the thirteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chola-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · State</span><span class="path-step-name">Chola Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A Tamil dynasty of southern India that built a powerful navy, and whose raids on Srivijaya's ports in the 11th century weakened its hold on maritime trade.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Chola Empire was a Tamil dynasty of southern India that reached its height in the eleventh century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/republic-of-venice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 697 CE – 1797 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Republic of Venice</span><span class="path-step-why">A merchant republic on the lagoon whose fleets and colonies, from Dalmatia to Crete and Cyprus, made it the main link between Latin Europe and the East.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/portuguese-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1415 CE – 1975 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Portuguese Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From Ceuta in 1415 a kingdom of perhaps a million people built the first sea-borne trading empire, opening the sea route to India with Vasco da Gama in 1498.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Portuguese Empire was one of the first European overseas empires, beginning with the capture of Ceuta in 1415 and lasting until the late twentieth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/dutch-republic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amsterdam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1581 CE – 1795 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Dutch Republic</span><span class="path-step-why">Its Baltic trade, its East India Company, founded in 1602, and the Amsterdam bank made it Europe's leading commercial power in the 17th century.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Dutch Republic was the federal state of the United Provinces of the Netherlands that emerged from the revolt against Spain in the late sixteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/british-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1583 CE – 1997 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">British Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The Royal Navy's victory at Trafalgar in 1805 secured a century of naval supremacy for the largest empire in history, which dissolved within decades after 1947.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The British Empire comprised the dominions, colonies, protectorates and other territories ruled or administered by Britain from the late sixteenth to the twentieth century.</span></span></a></li></ol>
